-- | OpenZeppelin Timelock - a mandatory delay between the proposal of a
-- privileged operation and its execution.
--
-- The Daml analogue of `TimelockController.sol`. On the EVM the timelock is a
-- contract that owns the protected contract and forwards calldata after a
-- delay. Daml has no dynamic dispatch and no calldata, so the operation is a
-- contract of its own: the consumer writes one template per operation kind,
-- with the typed parameters as fields, and gives it the `Timelocked` interface.
-- The protected template schedules such an operation in one choice and applies
-- it in another, and this package supplies the schedule computation and the
-- guards that make the delay hold.
--
-- # Authority comes from the protected contract
--
-- An operation contract is created inside a choice on the protected contract,
-- so it carries the signatures of that contract's signatories and nobody else
-- can forge one. The apply choice runs on the protected contract too, fetches
-- the operation the executor presents, checks that it targets this resource,
-- calls `requireReady`, archives it, and applies the typed parameters. The
-- only way to reach the privileged effect is through an operation that
-- matured, which is the whole guarantee.
--
-- The package names no proposer, executor, or canceller. Those are the
-- controllers of the consumer's own choices, and a role credential, a party
-- set, or an open executor all fit in a choice body, as `PausableV1` explains
-- for the pause authority.
--
-- # Time on Canton
--
-- A Daml transaction carries a ledger time that Canton checks against the
-- record time within a tolerance that the synchronizer configures. The guards
-- use the ledger-time bound primitives `isLedgerTimeGE` and `isLedgerTimeLT`,
-- so a check constrains only the bound that matters and does not pin the
-- ledger time to one instant. A submitter can move its ledger time inside the
-- tolerance, so a delay must be far longer than the tolerance for the
-- mechanism to mean anything; minutes at least, and days for governance.
--
-- Nothing executes on its own. Canton has no scheduled execution, so when an
-- operation is ready an executor must submit the apply choice. An automation
-- that watches the interface view and submits at `readyAt` is the consumer's
-- off-ledger responsibility.
--
-- # Scope
--
-- One operation is one contract and one apply choice. A Daml choice body is
-- atomic, so a batch is one operation template whose parameters list several
-- effects, applied in one choice. There is no predecessor field: an operation
-- that depends on another states the precondition it needs on the protected
-- contract's state, which is a stronger check than the identity of a
-- previously executed operation.
--
-- # Interfaces are frozen
--
-- Daml interfaces are not SCU-upgradeable, so this package defines one
-- interface and no templates. Operation templates live in consuming packages.
module OpenZeppelin.TimelockV1 where

import DA.Action (unless)
import DA.Fail (FailureCategory (..), FailureStatus (..), failWithStatus)
import DA.Optional (whenSome)
import qualified DA.TextMap as TextMap
import DA.Time (RelTime, addRelTime, isLedgerTimeGE, isLedgerTimeLE, isLedgerTimeLT)

-- | When a scheduled operation may execute.
data TimelockedView = TimelockedView with
    readyAt : Time
      -- ^ The earliest ledger time at which the operation may execute.
    expiresAt : Optional Time
      -- ^ The ledger time from which the operation may no longer execute, or
      --   `None` when the operation stays executable until it is archived.
  deriving (Eq, Show)

-- | A scheduled operation. Implement this on every template that represents an
-- operation waiting out its delay, whatever authority proposed it.
--
-- Signatories and observers: this interface adds none. It inherits whatever
--   the implementing template defines. An operation created in a choice on the
--   protected contract carries that contract's signatures.
-- Controllers: none. The interface declares no choice, because a choice needs
--   a controller expression drawn from the view and the view names no party.
-- Choices: none. Scheduling, executing, cancelling, and cleaning up are
--   choices the consumer writes.
-- Disclosure and privacy expectations: the pending operation is visible to
--   exactly the stakeholders of the implementing template. An executor that is
--   not a stakeholder receives the contract by explicit disclosure. Keep
--   sensitive parameters off a template that many parties observe.
-- Authorization assumptions: none. The interface confers no ability to
--   schedule or execute. Both need the protected contract's signatory
--   authority, which only a choice on the protected contract carries.
-- Archival behavior: none of its own. The consumer's apply choice archives the
--   operation when it executes, the cancel choice archives it early, and a
--   cleanup choice archives it after `expiresAt`.
-- Failure modes: none of its own. See the guards.
-- Upgrade and migration assumptions: frozen for the life of the package.
--   `TimelockedView` gains no fields, and this interface gains no methods. A
--   breaking change ships as a sibling `-v2` package.
interface Timelocked where
  viewtype TimelockedView

-- | The delay policy of a protected contract. Hold it as a field of the
-- template whose privileged choices the timelock guards, so that the policy is
-- part of the state that the schedule choice already exercises.
--
-- Changing the policy is itself a privileged operation. A consumer that wants
-- the `updateDelay` behavior of `TimelockController.sol` schedules the change
-- as an operation under the current policy, which is the self-administration
-- pattern the example package shows.
data TimelockConfig = TimelockConfig with
    minDelay : RelTime
      -- ^ The shortest delay a schedule may request. Zero disables the delay.
    gracePeriod : Optional RelTime
      -- ^ How long after `readyAt` an operation stays executable. `None`
      --   means an operation never expires, matching `TimelockController.sol`.
  deriving (Eq, Show)

-- | Compute the schedule of an operation that becomes ready at `readyAt`.
-- Call it in the choice that creates the operation contract and store the
-- returned fields on it:
--
-- > nonconsuming choice Treasury_ScheduleLimit : ContractId LimitChange
-- >   with newLimit : Decimal; readyAt : Time
-- >   controller proposer
-- >   do
-- >     s <- scheduleAt config readyAt
-- >     create LimitChange with
-- >       admin; treasuryId; newLimit
-- >       readyAt = s.readyAt; expiresAt = s.expiresAt
--
-- Fails with `eDelayTooShort` unless the ledger time is at least
-- `config.minDelay` before `readyAt`. The check is a ledger-time bound, so it
-- does not pin the transaction's ledger time.
scheduleAt : TimelockConfig -> Time -> Update TimelockedView
scheduleAt config readyAt = do
  early <- isLedgerTimeLE (addRelTime readyAt (negate config.minDelay))
  unless early (failWithStatus eDelayTooShort)
  pure TimelockedView with
    readyAt
    expiresAt = fmap (addRelTime readyAt) config.gracePeriod

-- | Compute the schedule of an operation that becomes ready `delay` after the
-- transaction's ledger time. It reads `getTime`, which pins the ledger time
-- of the transaction; use `scheduleAt` when the proposer knows the target time.
--
-- Fails with `eDelayTooShort` unless `delay` is at least `config.minDelay`.
scheduleAfter : TimelockConfig -> RelTime -> Update TimelockedView
scheduleAfter config delay = do
  unless (delay >= config.minDelay) (failWithStatus eDelayTooShort)
  now <- getTime
  let readyAt = addRelTime now delay
  pure TimelockedView with
    readyAt
    expiresAt = fmap (addRelTime readyAt) config.gracePeriod

-- | Fail unless the operation may execute now: the ledger time has reached
-- `readyAt` and, when `expiresAt` is set, has not reached it. Call it in the
-- apply choice after checking that the operation targets this resource, and
-- before applying its parameters:
--
-- > choice Treasury_ApplyLimit : ContractId Treasury
-- >   with opCid : ContractId LimitChange
-- >   controller executor
-- >   do
-- >     op <- fetch opCid
-- >     unless (op.admin == admin && op.treasuryId == treasuryId)
-- >       (failWithStatus eWrongTarget)
-- >     requireReady op
-- >     archive opCid
-- >     create this with limit = op.newLimit
--
-- Accepts an implementing template or a `Timelocked`. Fails with `eNotReady`
-- before `readyAt` and with `eExpired` at or after `expiresAt`.
requireReady : (HasToInterface t Timelocked) => t -> Update ()
requireReady x = do
  let v = view (toInterface @Timelocked x)
  ready <- isLedgerTimeGE v.readyAt
  unless ready (failWithStatus eNotReady)
  whenSome v.expiresAt $ \expiresAt -> do
    live <- isLedgerTimeLT expiresAt
    unless live (failWithStatus eExpired)

-- | Fail unless the operation has expired: `expiresAt` is set and the ledger
-- time has reached it. Use it on a cleanup choice that lets any party archive
-- an operation that can no longer execute.
--
-- Fails with `eNotExpired` when `expiresAt` is `None` or lies in the future.
requireExpired : (HasToInterface t Timelocked) => t -> Update ()
requireExpired x = do
  let v = view (toInterface @Timelocked x)
  case v.expiresAt of
    None -> failWithStatus eNotExpired
    Some expiresAt -> do
      expired <- isLedgerTimeGE expiresAt
      unless expired (failWithStatus eNotExpired)

-- | Whether the operation may execute at `now`: `readyAt <= now`, and
-- `now < expiresAt` when `expiresAt` is set. A pure function for a choice or
-- an off-ledger reader that branches rather than refuses.
isReadyAt : (HasToInterface t Timelocked) => Time -> t -> Bool
isReadyAt now x =
  let v = view (toInterface @Timelocked x)
  in v.readyAt <= now && optional True (now <) v.expiresAt

-- | Whether the operation has expired at `now`: `expiresAt` is set and
-- `expiresAt <= now`.
isExpiredAt : (HasToInterface t Timelocked) => Time -> t -> Bool
isExpiredAt now x =
  let v = view (toInterface @Timelocked x)
  in optional False (<= now) v.expiresAt

-- Failure statuses. Each is a `FailureStatus` raised with `failWithStatus`, so
-- a rejected submission reaches the Ledger API as `DAML_FAILURE` with the
-- `errorId` below, and a client matches on the id rather than on the message
-- text. In Daml Script, `trySubmit` returns it as `FailureStatusError`.

-- | Raised when a schedule requests a delay shorter than `minDelay`.
-- `errorId`: `openzeppelin.com/timelock-delay-too-short`.
eDelayTooShort : FailureStatus
eDelayTooShort = timelockFailure "delay-too-short" InvalidIndependentOfSystemState
  "Timelock: the delay is shorter than the minimum delay"

-- | Raised when an operation executes before `readyAt`.
-- `errorId`: `openzeppelin.com/timelock-not-ready`.
eNotReady : FailureStatus
eNotReady = timelockFailure "not-ready" InvalidGivenCurrentSystemStateOther
  "Timelock: the operation is not ready"

-- | Raised when an operation executes at or after `expiresAt`.
-- `errorId`: `openzeppelin.com/timelock-expired`.
eExpired : FailureStatus
eExpired = timelockFailure "expired" InvalidGivenCurrentSystemStateOther
  "Timelock: the operation has expired"

-- | Raised when a cleanup runs on an operation that has not expired.
-- `errorId`: `openzeppelin.com/timelock-not-expired`.
eNotExpired : FailureStatus
eNotExpired = timelockFailure "not-expired" InvalidGivenCurrentSystemStateOther
  "Timelock: the operation has not expired"

timelockFailure : Text -> FailureCategory -> Text -> FailureStatus
timelockFailure name category message = FailureStatus with
  errorId = "openzeppelin.com/timelock-" <> name
  category
  message
  meta = TextMap.empty
